occupationalSummary:
&lt;p&gt;This occupation is found in the transport sector. Rail is a vital component of the United Kingdoms infrastructure with train companies providing crucial rail services needed by passengers. Trains are also key to the delivery of freight across the nation, and specialist trains provide important rail network maintenance functions. Train companies operate both local, regional, national and international train services. Employers are therefore mainly large-sized commercial organisations. &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The broad purpose of the occupation is to drive trains in a safe, punctual, economic manner in accordance with rail rules, regulations and procedures.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A Train Driver could work in a number of rail environments, such as high speed, passenger, freight, underground, metro, suburban, cross border, depots, sidings or maintenance sheds; moving passengers, goods, empty coaching stock or driving on-track machines to perform infrastructure maintenance work.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A Train Driver will work shifts that include weekends, evenings and nights. On freight or engineering trains, train drivers usually tend to be rostered more night shifts. On long-distance routes, there may be a need for overnight stays. Train Drivers can be required to work for long periods of time on their own, they must maintain a high level of concentration and make instant complex decisions during normal and degraded conditions e.g. severe weather conditions, infrastructure failures and emergency working.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In their daily work, an employee in this occupation interacts with passengers, on-board staff, emergency services, the signaller, crossing attendant, operations control, other rail industry personnel or the British Transport and the civilian police. They must communicate clearly and accurately with stakeholders to provide a safe and effective rail operation that is also compliant with regulations.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An employee in this occupation will typically be responsible for passengers and/or goods on the train. Main duties typically include responsibility for ensuring the train enters and finishes service correctly and driving the train safely and to time. &amp;nbsp;It is crucial that Train Drivers know the features of the route they will be following and how to react to these features and also react to changing conditions. Train Drivers must ensure the correct protocol is followed consistently throughout the duration of the journey, for instance they must provide vital reports on train functions. They have a key role in keeping any passengers and other stakeholders up to date.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There are strict rules about who can be in the train drivers cab and so a train driver can expect to be physically apart from others onboard for much of the time. They hold a high degree of accountability for the safety and security of the train and those on board. They will typically work to a train scheduler who allocates their workload and a senior line manager whom they report into.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before completing the apprenticeship, all apprentices must attain their two-part train drivers licence, regulated by the Office of Rail and Road.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
